From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: forward Caps Lock as a key instead of syncing state

Applies after the Try Omarchy product-identity, dynamic-display,
immersive-mode and full-grab patches.

Upstream treats Caps Lock as state to reconcile rather than a key to
forward. Every event, mouse motion included, compares the host
NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock against QKBD_MOD_CAPSLOCK and taps
KEY_CAPSLOCK when they differ.

QKBD_MOD_CAPSLOCK is not the guest's state. ui/kbd-state.c flips that bit
only when a KEY_CAPSLOCK down passes through qkbd_state_key_event, never
reads it back from the guest, and never resets it. It records what QEMU
last sent. Any path that changes the guest's Caps Lock without going
through qkbd_state leaves the two permanently disagreeing, and the
comparison then reads as in sync, so QEMU never corrects it. Two such
paths matter here. virtio_input_send drops events while the guest driver
is unbound, so a sync sent during boot is lost while QEMU records it as
delivered. And the guest's own keymap can toggle Caps Lock on its own;
Omarchy binds both Shift keys together to Caps Lock.

The result is inverted capitals for the rest of the session, and it is
worse than inverted under Omarchy, which maps Caps Lock to Compose. There
a synthesized KEY_CAPSLOCK is not a lock toggle at all, it opens a compose
sequence that swallows the next keystrokes and emits some other symbol.
Guessed state then produces guest input the user never typed.

Forward the key instead. NSEventTypeFlagsChanged already carries
kVK_CapsLock, and the other modifiers are handled from that switch, so
handle Caps Lock the same way: one press and release per change of the lock
flag on an event the focused window actually received. The flag stays set
across the press and the release of the physical key, so testing it is what
keeps a single press from sending two taps and leaving the key inert.
Nothing is synthesized from guest state, so nothing can diverge, and a guest
that remaps the key gets exactly the presses the user made.

Caps Lock changed while the application is inactive is no longer replayed
on return. That matches the surrounding modifier handling, which the
upstream comment already describes as unreliable in that case, and it is
the honest outcome: the keystroke went to another application.

diff --git a/ui/cocoa.m b/ui/cocoa.m
--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -931,14 +931,8 @@
      * the application is inactive (i.e. no events fire) and we don't want to
      * wait for another modifier state change to detect such a change.
      *
-     * NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock requires a special treatment. The other flags
-     * are handled in similar manners.
-     *
-     * NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock
-     * ---------------------------
-     *
-     * If CapsLock state is changed, "up" and "down" events will be fired in
-     * sequence, effectively updates CapsLock state on the guest.
+     * NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock is deliberately not reconciled here. The
+     * key is forwarded from NSEventTypeFlagsChanged; see kVK_CapsLock below.
      *
      * The other flags
      * ---------------
@@ -957,12 +951,6 @@
      *   completely leaving your hands from the keyboard, which hopefully makes
      *   this implementation usable enough.
      */
-    if (!!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock) !=
-        qkbd_state_modifier_get(kbd, QKBD_MOD_CAPSLOCK)) {
-        qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, true);
-        qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, false);
-    }
-
     if (!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagShift)) {
         qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_LEFTSHIFT, false);
         qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_RIGHTSHIFT, false);
@@ -981,6 +969,34 @@
     switch ([event type]) {
         case NSEventTypeFlagsChanged:
             switch ([event keyCode]) {
+                /*
+                 * Caps Lock is a key, not a state to reconcile. QEMU cannot
+                 * read the guest's lock state back, so comparing against its
+                 * own record of what it last sent drives the guest wrong once
+                 * anything else changes it, and under a keymap that rebinds
+                 * Caps Lock a synthesized press is not a lock toggle at all.
+                 *
+                 * The lock flag stays set across the press and the release of
+                 * the physical key, so tap once per change of the flag rather
+                 * than once per event. The other cases in this switch test the
+                 * flag for the same reason. Comparing against the last flag
+                 * seen on a Caps Lock event, rather than against guest state,
+                 * keeps this reachable only from a real key the focused window
+                 * received. There is one view, so file scope would add nothing.
+                 */
+                case kVK_CapsLock: {
+                    static bool last_caps_lock_flag;
+                    bool caps_lock_flag =
+                        !!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock);
+
+                    if (caps_lock_flag != last_caps_lock_flag) {
+                        last_caps_lock_flag = caps_lock_flag;
+                        qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, true);
+                        qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, false);
+                    }
+                    break;
+                }
+
                 case kVK_Shift:
                     if (!!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagShift)) {
                         [self toggleKey:KEY_LEFTSHIFT];
